  "answer": {
    "text": "Audit active users: : Review your user list to identify inactive, terminated, or occasional users who still hold a paid license. Deactivating these accounts is the fastest way to trim the bill.\nDowngrade user tiers: : Check if everyone genuinely needs a full or Enterprise-level seat. Move light users (like people who only view reports or update basic fields) to lower-tier, cheaper, or free stakeholder/view-only licenses.\nConsolidate and clean data: : Merge duplicate contacts, archive dead leads, and purge bloated attachments or logs. Many CRMs charge based on storage tiers or database size alongside per-seat pricing.\nNegotiate volume discounting: : Talk to your account manager. Since your seat count has doubled, you have leverage to ask for a volume discount, a multi-year contract lock-in, or a price-per-seat cap.\nEvaluate alternative CRMs: : If your current provider refuses to budge on price and the cost-per-seat model is scaling unsustainably, look into competitors that offer flat-rate pricing, unlimited users, or cheaper entry tiers (e.g., migrating to a platform like HubSpot, ClickUp, or a more modular open-source CRM).",
